**Virtual functions: code**

    #include <iostream>
    #include <memory>
    #include <vector>
    using namespace std;

    class Shape {
    public:
        virtual double area() const = 0;       // pure virtual: Shape is abstract
        virtual ~Shape() = default;            // virtual destructor
    };
    class Rect : public Shape {
        double w, h;
    public:
        Rect(double w, double h) : w(w), h(h) {}
        double area() const override { return w * h; }
    };
    class Circle : public Shape {
        double r;
    public:
        explicit Circle(double r) : r(r) {}
        double area() const override { return 3.14159265 * r * r; }
    };

    int main() {
        vector<unique_ptr<Shape>> v;
        v.push_back(make_unique<Rect>(2, 3));
        v.push_back(make_unique<Circle>(1));
        for (auto& s : v) cout << s->area() << "\n";   // dynamic dispatch
    }

Explain: the base pointer calls the derived `area()` through the vtable; a class with a pure virtual function cannot be instantiated; `override` makes the compiler check the signature; the base destructor is virtual so deletion through a base pointer is safe.

**Operator overloading**

    struct Complex {
        double re, im;
        Complex operator+(const Complex& o) const { return {re + o.re, im + o.im}; }
        bool operator==(const Complex& o) const { return re == o.re && im == o.im; }
    };
    ostream& operator<<(ostream& os, const Complex& c) { return os << c.re << "+" << c.im << "i"; }

Rules: you cannot create new operators, nor overload `::`, `.`, `.*`, `?:` or `sizeof`; at least one operand must be a user-defined type; precedence and arity do not change. Binary operators are often members or free functions; `<<` and `>>` for streams must be free (often friend) functions because the left operand is the stream. Keep semantics intuitive; `operator=` and the copy constructor matter when the class owns resources (rule of three/five).

**MCQ traps:** calling a virtual function in a constructor, object slicing, default arguments in virtual functions, hiding versus overriding, `static` functions cannot be virtual, and constructors cannot be virtual.
